((window.CARDS_BY_LANG = window.CARDS_BY_LANG || {}).en = window.CARDS_BY_LANG.en || {}).company = [{"name":"Apple","emoji":"🍎","meta":["technology","USA","1976"],"description":"Founded by Steve Jobs, Steve Wozniak and Ronald Wayne, the company turned the personal computer from a curiosity into an everyday object. Its iPhone, Mac and iPad now set the tone for the whole electronics industry, and the bitten apple logo is recognised worldwide.","fact":"The first Apple logo showed Isaac Newton sitting under an apple tree — the bitten silhouette used today appeared only in 1977.","wiki_en":"Apple Inc."},{"name":"Microsoft","emoji":"🪟","meta":["technology","USA","1975"],"description":"Bill Gates and Paul Allen founded the company that made Windows the standard operating system for home and office computers. Microsoft is also known today for the Office suite, the Xbox gaming platform and its Azure cloud service.","fact":"The name Microsoft is a blend of \"microcomputer\" and \"software\", and the partners' first product was a BASIC interpreter for the Altair computer.","wiki_en":"Microsoft"},{"name":"Google","emoji":"🔍","meta":["technology","USA","1998"],"description":"Larry Page and Sergey Brin started with a search algorithm they came up with at Stanford, then built one of the world's largest technology companies around it. Its products now include Gmail, the video platform YouTube and the Android operating system.","fact":"The name Google came out of a misspelling of \"googol\", the mathematicians' word for a one followed by a hundred zeros.","wiki_en":"Google"},{"name":"Amazon","emoji":"📦","meta":["e-commerce","USA","1994"],"description":"Jeff Bezos began by selling books online out of his own garage and turned the venture into the largest marketplace in the world. The company also owns the cloud service AWS, which keeps a sizeable share of the internet running.","fact":"Amazon was named after the river, one of the mightiest on Earth — a hint at the scale of the catalogue.","wiki_en":"Amazon (company)"},{"name":"Meta","emoji":"♾️","meta":["technology","USA","2004"],"description":"Mark Zuckerberg launched Facebook as a student social network at Harvard, and it grew into one of the largest media platforms in the world. In 2021 the company renamed itself Meta, gathering Facebook, Instagram and WhatsApp under one name.","fact":"The new name and the infinity-loop logo were meant to underline the company's bet on virtual and augmented reality.","wiki_en":"Meta Platforms"},{"name":"Tesla","emoji":"⚡","meta":["automotive","USA","2003"],"description":"Named after the inventor Nikola Tesla, the company was among the first to prove that electric cars could be fast and desirable rather than just a green compromise. Elon Musk took charge early on and turned it into a symbol of electric transport.","fact":"Tesla's first model was a roadster built on the body of the Lotus Elise — back then the company did not yet make platforms of its own.","wiki_en":"Tesla, Inc."},{"name":"Nvidia","emoji":"🎮","meta":["technology","USA","1993"],"description":"The company began by making graphics cards for gaming computers, and its chips became a key ingredient of modern graphics and video games. In recent years Nvidia has turned into the leading supplier of processors for artificial intelligence systems.","fact":"The word Nvidia comes from the Latin invidia, \"envy\": the co-founders wanted competitors to envy their product.","wiki_en":"Nvidia"},{"name":"OpenAI","emoji":"🤖","meta":["technology","USA","2015"],"description":"A research lab set up as a non-profit to develop artificial intelligence safely, it later changed its structure and released the chatbot ChatGPT. The service became one of the fastest-growing in the history of the internet.","fact":"Elon Musk was among the founders of OpenAI; he later left the project and became one of the company's critics.","wiki_en":"OpenAI"},{"name":"Netflix","emoji":"🍿","meta":["media","USA","1997"],"description":"The company started out mailing DVDs to subscribers, then became the first major player to bet on streaming video by subscription. Netflix is now one of the world's biggest producers and distributors of series and films.","fact":"The first film the company mailed to a customer was \"Beetlejuice\" on DVD, back in 1998.","wiki_en":"Netflix"},{"name":"The Walt Disney Company","emoji":"🏰","meta":["media","USA","1923"],"description":"Founded by the brothers Walt and Roy Disney as a small animation studio, the company grew into one of the largest media corporations in the world. It now owns film studios, theme parks, television channels and the streaming service Disney+.","fact":"The studio's first truly successful feature-length cartoon was \"Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs\" in 1937.","wiki_en":"The Walt Disney Company"},{"name":"Coca-Cola","emoji":"🥤","meta":["beverages","USA","1892"],"description":"Created as a pharmacy syrup by the chemist John Pemberton, the drink became one of the most recognisable brands on the planet. The formula of the syrup is treated as a trade secret and kept under tight wraps.","fact":"The classic ribbed Coca-Cola bottle was designed in 1915 so that it could be recognised by touch alone, even in the dark.","wiki_en":"The Coca-Cola Company"},{"name":"PepsiCo","emoji":"🥤","meta":["beverages","USA","1965"],"description":"The company was formed by the merger of the maker of Pepsi-Cola with the snack producer Frito-Lay, putting fizzy drinks and crisps under one roof. Its portfolio now holds dozens of food and drink brands around the world.","fact":"The name Pepsi points to the enzyme pepsin — the inventor of the drink first advertised it as a remedy for an upset stomach.","wiki_en":"PepsiCo"},{"name":"McDonald's","emoji":"🍟","meta":["restaurants","USA","1940"],"description":"The McDonald brothers' restaurant in California became the model for a fast-food system that the entrepreneur Ray Kroc then rolled out across the country. The golden arches are now one of the most recognisable symbols in the world.","fact":"McDonald's began as an ordinary roadside barbecue joint; the assembly-line service system arrived only when it reopened in 1948.","wiki_en":"McDonald's"},{"name":"Starbucks","emoji":"☕","meta":["food service","USA","1971"],"description":"A small Seattle shop selling roasted beans grew into the world's largest coffee-house chain under Howard Schultz, who brought the feel of Italian espresso bars into it. The green siren logo now turns up in almost every big city.","fact":"Starbucks takes its name from the first mate in the novel \"Moby-Dick\".","wiki_en":"Starbucks"},{"name":"KFC","emoji":"🍗","meta":["food service","USA","1930"],"description":"The fast-food chain grew out of a roadside restaurant run by Colonel Harland Sanders, who made his name with fried chicken cooked to a house recipe of eleven herbs and spices. The recipe is still kept secret.","fact":"The portrait of Colonel Sanders on the packaging is a real man, who promoted the brand in person well into old age.","wiki_en":"KFC"},{"name":"Burger King","emoji":"🍔","meta":["food service","USA","1953"],"description":"The fast-food chain made the Whopper its signature burger and spent decades competing with McDonald's for the title of the world's biggest hamburger chain. Its trademark is grilling the patties over an open flame.","fact":"The company was originally called Insta-Burger King, but after financial trouble its franchisees bought it out and shortened the name.","wiki_en":"Burger King"},{"name":"Subway","emoji":"🥪","meta":["food service","USA","1965"],"description":"The sandwich chain started with a single shop opened by 17-year-old Fred DeLuca on money borrowed from a family friend. Its franchised outlets now operate in almost every country, letting customers build a sandwich to their own taste.","fact":"The company's first shop was called Pete's Super Submarines — the name Subway came only a few years later.","wiki_en":"Subway (restaurant)"},{"name":"Nike","emoji":"👟","meta":["sportswear","USA","1964"],"description":"The company grew out of a small distributor of Japanese running shoes founded by the track coach Bill Bowerman and his former student Phil Knight. Both the name and the tick-shaped logo nod to the ancient Greek goddess of victory.","fact":"The famous Swoosh logo was drawn by a design student, Carolyn Davidson, for just 35 dollars.","wiki_en":"Nike, Inc."},{"name":"Adidas","emoji":"⚽","meta":["sportswear","Germany","1949"],"description":"Adi Dassler founded the company, whose name comes from his nickname and surname, after parting ways with his brother Rudi, who set up the rival Puma. The three stripes went on to become one of the most recognisable marks in sport.","fact":"The Dassler brothers never made peace: their family split divided their home town of Herzogenaurach into two camps loyal to the rival brands.","wiki_en":"Adidas"},{"name":"Puma","emoji":"🐾","meta":["sportswear","Germany","1948"],"description":"Rudolf Dassler set the company up after quarrelling with his brother Adi and leaving the family business that eventually became Adidas. The two brands have been competing in sportswear and footwear for decades since.","fact":"The leaping puma logo appeared on the company's products in the 1960s and has barely changed since.","wiki_en":"Puma (brand)"},{"name":"Zara","emoji":"👗","meta":["clothing","Spain","1975"],"description":"Amancio Ortega's Spanish label became the emblem of fast fashion: new collections are designed and reach the shops within weeks rather than the months rivals need. The chain is now part of the Inditex group.","fact":"The first shop opened in A Coruña under this name after the founder dropped his first choice, Zorba, because the trademark was already taken.","wiki_en":"Zara (retailer)"},{"name":"H&M","emoji":"👚","meta":["clothing","Sweden","1947"],"description":"The Swedish clothing chain began as a single womenswear boutique called Hennes in Västerås, and took its present name after buying a hunting-goods shop named Mauritz. H&M is now one of the largest mass-market chains in the world.","fact":"H&M stands for Hennes & Mauritz — the names of the two brands that came together.","wiki_en":"H&M"},{"name":"Uniqlo","emoji":"🧥","meta":["clothing","Japan","1949"],"description":"The Japanese brand grew out of a family menswear shop in Yamaguchi Prefecture, and the first store under the Uniqlo sign opened in Hiroshima in 1984. It is known for plain but technical clothes such as HEATTECH thermal wear, and belongs to the Fast Retailing group.","fact":"Uniqlo is short for Unique Clothing Warehouse, and a clerical slip when registering the brand in Hong Kong fixed the spelling with a Q.","wiki_en":"Uniqlo"},{"name":"IKEA","emoji":"🛋️","meta":["retail","Sweden","1943"],"description":"Ingvar Kamprad founded the company at seventeen in a Swedish village, and furniture packed flat for the customer to assemble became its signature trick, cutting the cost of shipping and storage. IKEA stores, with their maze of showrooms and Swedish meatballs, now operate worldwide.","fact":"The name IKEA is made of the founder's initials plus the names of the farm and village he grew up on — Elmtaryd and Agunnaryd.","wiki_en":"IKEA"},{"name":"LEGO","emoji":"🧱","meta":["toys","Denmark","1932"],"description":"The Danish carpenter Ole Kirk Christiansen started with wooden toys, and the company patented its famous interlocking plastic bricks in 1958. The brick has barely changed since and still fits parts made half a century ago.","fact":"The name LEGO is made from the Danish words leg godt — \"play well\".","wiki_en":"Lego"},{"name":"Louis Vuitton","emoji":"👜","meta":["fashion and luxury","France","1854"],"description":"The French house began as a workshop making travelling trunks for Parisian high society, and its monogram pattern was devised to fight off counterfeits. It is now one of the most valuable fashion houses in the world.","fact":"Unlike its rivals' rounded lids, Louis Vuitton's flat-topped trunks were designed so that they could be stacked in transit.","wiki_en":"Louis Vuitton"},{"name":"Gucci","emoji":"👛","meta":["fashion and luxury","Italy","1921"],"description":"Guccio Gucci founded the Italian fashion house in Florence, starting with leather goods for travellers inspired by his years working in London hotels. The signature green-and-red stripe recalls the riding tack the brand was known for.","fact":"The Gucci family's story is as famous as the goods: the feuds among the heirs became the basis for the Hollywood film \"House of Gucci\".","wiki_en":"Gucci"},{"name":"Chanel","emoji":"👗","meta":["fashion and luxury","France","1910"],"description":"The house was founded by Coco Chanel, who freed women's fashion from corsets and made the little black dress and the tweed suit everyday wear. The scent Chanel No. 5 became one of the most famous perfumes ever made.","fact":"Asked what she wore to bed, Marilyn Monroe is said to have answered: a few drops of Chanel No. 5.","wiki_en":"Chanel"},{"name":"Hermès","emoji":"🧣","meta":["fashion and luxury","France","1837"],"description":"The French house started out as a workshop for harnesses and saddles and over time moved on to handbags, silk scarves and other luxuries. The Birkin bag, named after the actress Jane Birkin, became one of the emblems of the brand.","fact":"A single Hermès Birkin is stitched by hand by one craftsman over many hours, and the queue to buy one can stretch for years.","wiki_en":"Hermès"},{"name":"Rolex","emoji":"⌚","meta":["watchmaking","Switzerland","1905"],"description":"The watch company was founded in London by the German entrepreneur Hans Wilsdorf, who later moved production to Geneva. Rolex was among the first to make waterproof, self-winding wristwatches, and its name became a byword for status.","fact":"In 1927 an Oyster spent hours in the water on the wrist of the swimmer Mercedes Gleitze during a Channel crossing and kept running — the company advertised it loudly.","wiki_en":"Rolex"},{"name":"Samsung","emoji":"📱","meta":["technology","South Korea","1938"],"description":"The company began as a small trading firm supplying groceries and dried fruit, and after the Korean War it moved step by step into electronics, becoming one of the world's largest makers of smartphones, televisions and microchips.","fact":"Samsung means \"three stars\" in Korean — a symbol of greatness, abundance and eternity chosen by the company's founder.","wiki_en":"Samsung Electronics"},{"name":"Sony","emoji":"🎮","meta":["technology","Japan","1946"],"description":"The company appeared in war-ruined Tokyo as a tiny electronics repair shop and made its name with pocket transistor radios and the Walkman. Sony is also known today for its PlayStation consoles and for making films.","fact":"The name Sony was coined from the Latin sonus, \"sound\", and the English \"sonny\", a word for a lively young lad.","wiki_en":"Sony"},{"name":"Nintendo","emoji":"🍄","meta":["video games","Japan","1889"],"description":"The company started out making hanafuda playing cards in Kyoto and moved into video games almost a century later, giving the world Mario and the NES console. Nintendo remains one of the most influential game developers today.","fact":"Nintendo is older than most familiar technology companies: it is over 130 years old, and in that time it changed its line of business several times.","wiki_en":"Nintendo"},{"name":"LG","emoji":"📺","meta":["technology","South Korea","1947"],"description":"The South Korean company grew from a maker of cosmetics and plastics into one of the world's largest groups in consumer electronics and appliances. The name LG comes from the initials of the two brands that merged, Lucky and Goldstar.","fact":"The slogan \"Life's Good\", which the initials LG are now made to fit, appeared long after the name had been chosen for quite different reasons.","wiki_en":"LG Corporation"},{"name":"Panasonic","emoji":"🔌","meta":["technology","Japan","1918"],"description":"Konosuke Matsushita founded the company making sockets for light bulbs and went on to build one of the world's largest consumer electronics groups. For a long time the brand was known as Matsushita, until it settled on the single name Panasonic in 2008.","fact":"Konosuke Matsushita, the founder, wrote books on business philosophy and is regarded in Japan as one of the fathers of modern management.","wiki_en":"Panasonic"},{"name":"Canon","emoji":"📷","meta":["technology","Japan","1937"],"description":"The Japanese company started out copying German Leica cameras under the name Kwanon, after the Buddhist goddess of mercy. Canon is today one of the world's leading makers of cameras, lenses and office equipment.","fact":"The company's first camera prototype was called Kwanon and carried an image of the thousand-armed goddess of mercy on its body.","wiki_en":"Canon Inc."},{"name":"Toyota","emoji":"🚗","meta":["automotive","Japan","1937"],"description":"The company grew out of a division of Toyoda, a maker of textile looms; the altered spelling Toyota proved easier to write in Japanese and kinder to the ear. Today it is one of the world's largest carmakers, famous for its lean production system.","fact":"The name was changed from Toyoda to Toyota partly because the new spelling takes eight brush strokes instead of ten — a lucky number in Japan.","wiki_en":"Toyota"},{"name":"Honda","emoji":"🏍️","meta":["automotive","Japan","1948"],"description":"The self-taught engineer Soichiro Honda founded the company by fitting engines to bicycles in post-war Japan, then moved on to motorcycles and cars. Honda is still the world's largest maker of internal combustion engines by number of units built.","fact":"Soichiro Honda never earned an engineering degree — he tested every idea in practice, which earned him a name as a genius with a mechanic's hands.","wiki_en":"Honda"},{"name":"Hyundai","emoji":"🚙","meta":["automotive","South Korea","1967"],"description":"The car arm of the South Korean Hyundai conglomerate began by assembling Ford models under licence before moving on to designs of its own. Hyundai Motor is now one of the world's largest carmakers and the owner of the Kia brand.","fact":"The word hyundai means \"modernity\" in Korean — the group's founder Chung Ju-yung chose it for the parent construction company.","wiki_en":"Hyundai Motor Company"},{"name":"Volkswagen","emoji":"🐞","meta":["automotive","Germany","1937"],"description":"Volkswagen means \"the people's car\": the company was set up in Germany to mass-produce an affordable vehicle designed by Ferdinand Porsche, later known as the Beetle. After the war the group became one of the world's largest carmakers.","fact":"The company's plant in Wolfsburg was built around a single model — the Beetle, which stayed in production for over half a century with only minor changes.","wiki_en":"Volkswagen"},{"name":"BMW","emoji":"🏎️","meta":["automotive","Germany","1916"],"description":"Bayerische Motoren Werke began by making aircraft engines and turned to cars only in the 1920s. The round white-and-blue badge was long mistaken for a spinning propeller, though it in fact echoes the colours of Bavaria.","fact":"Despite the popular propeller legend, the company itself confirmed the Bavarian colours as the source of the badge only decades later.","wiki_en":"BMW"},{"name":"Mercedes-Benz","emoji":"⭐","meta":["automotive","Germany","1926"],"description":"The brand came out of the merger of the companies of Karl Benz, who built the world's first car with an internal combustion engine, and Gottlieb Daimler. The three-pointed star stands for the founders' ambition to build engines for land, water and air.","fact":"Karl Benz and Gottlieb Daimler worked independently and never met in person, even though their companies later merged.","wiki_en":"Mercedes-Benz"},{"name":"Ferrari","emoji":"🐎","meta":["automotive","Italy","1939"],"description":"Enzo Ferrari set up the company after leaving the Alfa Romeo racing team, betting on sports cars for competition and for wealthy collectors. The black prancing horse on a yellow field became one of the most recognisable logos in motorsport.","fact":"Enzo Ferrari took the prancing horse from the family of Francesco Baracca, a First World War flying ace who painted the symbol on his plane.","wiki_en":"Ferrari"},{"name":"Porsche","emoji":"🏎️","meta":["automotive","Germany","1931"],"description":"Ferdinand Porsche founded an engineering studio that designed cars for other marques, the people's Volkswagen among them, before building sports cars under its own name. The 911 has been the company's calling card for over half a century.","fact":"The first car to carry the Porsche name, the 356, was put together largely by hand by the founder's son Ferry Porsche in an Austrian workshop.","wiki_en":"Porsche"},{"name":"Ford","emoji":"🏭","meta":["automotive","USA","1903"],"description":"Henry Ford did not invent the car, but his moving assembly line made it affordable for ordinary buyers rather than just the rich. The Model T, built for almost twenty years with barely a change, became the emblem of that revolution.","fact":"Ford is said to have offered the Model T in any colour, so long as it was black: the palette was cut back for years to keep the line moving fast.","wiki_en":"Ford Motor Company"},{"name":"General Motors","emoji":"🛻","meta":["automotive","USA","1908"],"description":"The company brought several independent car marques under one roof, among them Buick, Cadillac and Chevrolet, creating one of the world's first multi-brand car corporations. For a long time General Motors was the largest carmaker on the planet.","fact":"The founder, William Durant, lost control of the company twice and was pushed out of the leadership by its own board of directors.","wiki_en":"General Motors"},{"name":"Boeing","emoji":"✈️","meta":["aviation","USA","1916"],"description":"The company was founded by the timber merchant William Boeing, who caught the flying bug and built his first seaplane in Seattle. Boeing is now one of the two largest makers of passenger airliners in the world, alongside Europe's Airbus.","fact":"The company's first product was the B&W, a wooden seaplane built in a former boatyard.","wiki_en":"Boeing"},{"name":"Airbus","emoji":"🛩️","meta":["aviation","France","1970"],"description":"The European aircraft consortium was created by several countries so that together they could take on the American planemakers, above all Boeing. Airbus builds both the narrow-body A320 family and the A380, the largest passenger aircraft in series production.","fact":"Airbus parts are made in factories across Europe and brought together for final assembly — often by air, aboard the special Beluga transport planes.","wiki_en":"Airbus"},{"name":"SpaceX","emoji":"🚀","meta":["aerospace","USA","2002"],"description":"Elon Musk founded the company to make spaceflight cheaper and, in the end, to make settling Mars possible. SpaceX was the first in the world to bring rocket stages back and fly them again, which cut the cost of launches sharply.","fact":"The first three launches of the Falcon 1 ended in failure and the company nearly went bankrupt before the fourth attempt finally worked.","wiki_en":"SpaceX"},{"name":"Intel","emoji":"🔲","meta":["technology","USA","1968"],"description":"The company was founded by the engineers Robert Noyce and Gordon Moore, the author of Moore's law on the regular doubling of transistors on a chip. For decades Intel remained the main supplier of processors for personal computers.","fact":"The \"Intel Inside\" slogan and the five-note jingle that played as computers started up became one of the most recognisable sound trademarks in history.","wiki_en":"Intel"},{"name":"AMD","emoji":"🔶","meta":["technology","USA","1969"],"description":"Advanced Micro Devices appeared as an alternative chip supplier and spent years as the perennial runner-up to Intel on the processor market. In recent years AMD has gained ground thanks to its successful Ryzen processors and Radeon graphics cards.","fact":"AMD's founder Jerry Sanders had earlier worked at Fairchild Semiconductor alongside the future founders of Intel.","wiki_en":"AMD"},{"name":"IBM","emoji":"🖥️","meta":["technology","USA","1911"],"description":"The company was formed by the merger of several makers of scales, clocks and tabulating machines long before computers existed, and took the name International Business Machines in 1924. IBM pioneered mainframes and personal computers, though it later sold the PC arm to Lenovo.","fact":"It was IBM's Deep Blue that in 1997 became the first machine to beat a reigning world chess champion, Garry Kasparov, in a full match.","wiki_en":"IBM"},{"name":"Oracle","emoji":"🗄️","meta":["technology","USA","1977"],"description":"The programmer Larry Ellison founded the company after an IBM engineer's paper on relational databases inspired him to build a commercial product on the idea before IBM itself did. Oracle is now one of the world's largest suppliers of database and business software.","fact":"The name Oracle was inherited from the code name of a classified CIA project the founders had worked on before starting a business of their own.","wiki_en":"Oracle Corporation"},{"name":"HP","emoji":"🖨️","meta":["technology","USA","1939"],"description":"Bill Hewlett and Dave Packard started out in a Palo Alto garage — later called the cradle of Silicon Valley — making electronic measuring instruments. The company later became known above all for its computers and printers.","fact":"The garage where Hewlett and Packard built their first instrument is now a California state historic landmark, marked as the birthplace of Silicon Valley.","wiki_en":"HP Inc."},{"name":"Adobe","emoji":"🎨","meta":["technology","USA","1982"],"description":"The company was founded by former Xerox engineers who had developed PostScript, a page description language that revolutionised desktop printing and layout. Adobe later became known for the Photoshop editor and the PDF format.","fact":"Adobe took its name from Adobe Creek, the stream that ran behind the house of one of the founders in California.","wiki_en":"Adobe Inc."},{"name":"Nokia","emoji":"📞","meta":["technology","Finland","1865"],"description":"The Finnish company began as a small paper mill and took its name from the town of Nokia on the Nokianvirta river. Over a century and a half it made rubber, cables and televisions before becoming the world's biggest mobile phone maker in the 1990s and 2000s.","fact":"The Nokia Tune ringtone, familiar to millions of button-phone owners, is a fragment of a guitar piece by the Spanish composer Francisco Tárrega.","wiki_en":"Nokia"},{"name":"Siemens","emoji":"🔧","meta":["technology","Germany","1847"],"description":"The engineer Werner von Siemens founded the company together with the mechanic Johann Halske, starting with telegraph equipment. Siemens is now one of Europe's largest industrial groups, working in energy, transport and medical equipment.","fact":"Siemens laid one of the first transcontinental telegraph lines, linking London with Calcutta back in the 19th century.","wiki_en":"Siemens"},{"name":"Bosch","emoji":"🔩","meta":["technology","Germany","1886"],"description":"Robert Bosch opened a workshop for precision mechanics and electrical engineering in Stuttgart, and ignition systems for car engines brought the company worldwide fame. Bosch today makes everything from household appliances to car components.","fact":"Bosch is owned largely by the Robert Bosch charitable foundation rather than by private shareholders — a decision made by the founder himself.","wiki_en":"Robert Bosch GmbH"},{"name":"Philips","emoji":"💡","meta":["technology","Netherlands","1891"],"description":"Gerard Philips founded the company with his father, starting out making carbon-filament light bulbs in Eindhoven. Philips later expanded into consumer electronics, medical equipment and lighting, and remains one of Europe's largest technology groups.","fact":"It was Philips engineers, together with Sony, who developed the compact disc in the early 1980s — the standard for storing music for decades.","wiki_en":"Philips"},{"name":"Uber","emoji":"🚕","meta":["technology","USA","2009"],"description":"The idea for the ride-hailing app came after its founders failed to find a taxi in Paris and decided to make a car appear at the touch of a button. Uber changed the transport market worldwide and set off a whole wave of similar services.","fact":"The service was first called UberCab and could only summon black premium limousines, not ordinary cars.","wiki_en":"Uber"},{"name":"Airbnb","emoji":"🏠","meta":["technology","USA","2008"],"description":"The service started when its founders rented out air mattresses in their own San Francisco flat during a conference, when the city had run out of hotel rooms. That grew into a platform for short-term lets that changed the travel market.","fact":"The name Airbnb is short for AirBed & Breakfast — after those very first air mattresses.","wiki_en":"Airbnb"},{"name":"Spotify","emoji":"🎧","meta":["technology","Sweden","2006"],"description":"The Swedish entrepreneurs Daniel Ek and Martin Lorentzon built a subscription music service as a legal answer to the pirate downloading that was then flooding the industry. Spotify is now one of the world's largest streaming services for music and podcasts.","fact":"Spotify Wrapped, the yearly round-up of a listener's most-played tracks, has become one of the most talked-about things on social media every December.","wiki_en":"Spotify"},{"name":"PayPal","emoji":"💳","meta":["fintech","USA","1998"],"description":"The online payments service came out of a merger of companies whose founders included Elon Musk, Peter Thiel and other future stars of Silicon Valley, later nicknamed the PayPal Mafia. PayPal was among the first mass ways to send money over the internet and is still widely used in online shops.","fact":"Many early PayPal employees went on to found or lead Tesla, SpaceX, YouTube and LinkedIn — hence the nickname PayPal Mafia.","wiki_en":"PayPal"},{"name":"Visa","emoji":"🏧","meta":["fintech","USA","1958"],"description":"The payment network grew out of the credit card programme of Bank of America and only later became an independent company under its present name. Visa cards are accepted at millions of places worldwide, and the company runs the payment plumbing between banks rather than lending money itself.","fact":"The word visa was chosen as a name precisely because it is pronounced and recognised in much the same way in almost every language.","wiki_en":"Visa Inc."},{"name":"Mastercard","emoji":"🌐","meta":["fintech","USA","1966"],"description":"The company began as an alliance of several American banks that wanted a shared credit card system to compete with Bank of America. Its logo of two overlapping circles has stayed almost unchanged for decades.","fact":"The association was originally called Interbank and took the name Mastercard only in 1979.","wiki_en":"Mastercard"},{"name":"JPMorgan Chase","emoji":"🏦","meta":["finance","USA","2000"],"description":"Today's bank was formed by the merger of J. P. Morgan's investment house, which helped rescue the American economy during the panics of the early 20th century, with the commercial Chase Manhattan Bank. It is now one of the largest banks in the world by assets.","fact":"John Pierpont Morgan personally organised the rescue of the American financial system during the panic of 1907, before the Federal Reserve existed.","wiki_en":"JPMorgan Chase"},{"name":"Goldman Sachs","emoji":"💹","meta":["finance","USA","1869"],"description":"The investment bank was founded by Marcus Goldman, an immigrant from Germany, who began by trading commercial paper on the streets of New York. Over time the firm grew into one of the most influential investment banks on Wall Street.","fact":"The name gained its second half only after the founder's son-in-law, Samuel Sachs, joined the business.","wiki_en":"Goldman Sachs"},{"name":"Berkshire Hathaway","emoji":"💰","meta":["investment","USA","1839"],"description":"The company started as a New England textile mill; in the 1960s the young investor Warren Buffett bought up its shares and gradually turned a loss-making factory into a holding vehicle for his investments. Berkshire Hathaway now holds stakes in dozens of companies, from insurers to railways.","fact":"Buffett later called buying the textile mill one of the worst investment mistakes of his career, even though it gave his whole empire its name.","wiki_en":"Berkshire Hathaway"},{"name":"BlackRock","emoji":"📈","meta":["investment","USA","1988"],"description":"The company began as a small risk management unit inside an investment bank and grew into the largest asset manager in the world. It now looks after trillions of dollars belonging to pension funds, companies and private investors.","fact":"BlackRock's analytics platform, Aladdin, is used not only by the firm itself but by many other large financial institutions around the world.","wiki_en":"BlackRock"},{"name":"Walmart","emoji":"🛒","meta":["retail","USA","1962"],"description":"Sam Walton opened his first shop in a small Arkansas town, betting on low prices and high volumes in rural areas the big retailers were in no hurry to enter. Walmart is now the world's largest company by revenue and one of the planet's biggest employers.","fact":"Sam Walton toured competitors' shops in person with a notebook, jotting down good ideas and then putting them to work in his own chain.","wiki_en":"Walmart"},{"name":"Alibaba","emoji":"🛍️","meta":["e-commerce","China","1999"],"description":"A former English teacher, Jack Ma, gathered a team of like minds in his flat in Hangzhou and built a site for trade between companies, which grew into China's largest e-commerce ecosystem. Alibaba now brings together online retail, cloud computing and digital payments.","fact":"The name came from the tale of Ali Baba and the Forty Thieves — Jack Ma liked that the word is easy to say and understood in many countries.","wiki_en":"Alibaba Group"},{"name":"Tencent","emoji":"🐧","meta":["technology","China","1998"],"description":"The company started with the QQ messenger, one of the main ways to talk online in China, and then built WeChat around it — an app that folds messaging, payments and services for every occasion into one. Tencent is also one of the world's biggest publishers of and investors in video games.","fact":"The cheerful penguin in a scarf, mascot of the QQ messenger, is still the company's emblem, long after its business outgrew chat.","wiki_en":"Tencent"},{"name":"ByteDance","emoji":"🎵","meta":["technology","China","2012"],"description":"The company built the algorithms behind the news aggregator Toutiao and then applied the same approach to recommending short videos in the app Douyin, known outside China as TikTok. The personalised feed made the service one of the fastest-growing in the world.","fact":"TikTok and its Chinese twin Douyin formally run as separate apps on separate servers, even though they belong to the same company.","wiki_en":"ByteDance"},{"name":"Huawei","emoji":"📡","meta":["technology","China","1987"],"description":"Ren Zhengfei, a former officer in the Chinese army, started out reselling telephone switchboards and built one of the world's largest makers of telecommunications equipment and smartphones. The company played a key part in rolling out 5G networks worldwide.","fact":"The name Huawei can be read as \"Chinese achievement\" or \"splendid act\" — it carries a patriotic ring.","wiki_en":"Huawei"},{"name":"Xiaomi","emoji":"🍚","meta":["technology","China","2010"],"description":"The entrepreneur Lei Jun founded the company betting on smartphones with strong specifications at low prices, sold mostly online to skip retail mark-ups. Xiaomi now makes not only phones but smart appliances, gadgets and even electric cars.","fact":"Xiaomi means \"little millet\" — a grain that in Chinese culture stands for a modest start to a great undertaking.","wiki_en":"Xiaomi"},{"name":"BYD","emoji":"🔋","meta":["automotive","China","1995"],"description":"The company started out making batteries for mobile phones and later turned the same know-how to electric cars, gradually outselling many of its global rivals. The name BYD stands for Build Your Dreams.","fact":"Warren Buffett, through his Berkshire Hathaway holding, was one of the first big Western investors in BYD, long before the company was known outside China.","wiki_en":"BYD Company"},{"name":"Shell","emoji":"🐚","meta":["oil and gas","United Kingdom","1907"],"description":"The company grew out of a London antiques shop that also traded in sea shells, which is where its name and logo come from. Over time Shell became one of the largest oil and gas corporations in the world.","fact":"The founders' family business really did begin with selling decorative sea shells brought from the Far East.","wiki_en":"Shell plc"},{"name":"ExxonMobil","emoji":"⛽","meta":["oil and gas","USA","1999"],"description":"The company was formed by the merger of Exxon and Mobil, the two largest heirs of John Rockefeller's Standard Oil empire, which an antitrust ruling had split apart back in the early 20th century. ExxonMobil is today one of the world's biggest oil and gas groups.","fact":"Both Exxon and Mobil trace their history to the same company, Standard Oil, broken up by the US authorities in 1911 for its monopoly position.","wiki_en":"ExxonMobil"},{"name":"Saudi Aramco","emoji":"🛢️","meta":["oil and gas","Saudi Arabia","1933"],"description":"The company began as a concession granted to American oilmen in the Saudi deserts, was later nationalised in full and became the kingdom's state oil monopoly. Aramco manages some of the world's largest oil reserves and remains one of the most profitable companies on the planet.","fact":"Saudi Aramco's share offering in 2019 was the largest IPO in the history of the world's stock markets.","wiki_en":"Saudi Aramco"},{"name":"BP","emoji":"🌻","meta":["oil and gas","United Kingdom","1909"],"description":"The company was created as Anglo-Persian Oil to develop the Iranian oil fields found by British concession holders. After several changes of name it became known as British Petroleum, and later shortened that to BP.","fact":"The green-and-yellow sunflower logo was adopted in 2000 along with the slogan \"Beyond Petroleum\", signalling a bet on alternative energy.","wiki_en":"BP"},{"name":"Nestlé","emoji":"🍫","meta":["food","Switzerland","1866"],"description":"The company was founded by the pharmacist Henri Nestlé, who developed an infant milk formula for babies who could not be fed by their mothers. Nestlé is today the largest food company in the world, with hundreds of brands from coffee to chocolate.","fact":"The founder's surname means \"little nest\" in German, which is why a bird's nest still appears on the company's logo.","wiki_en":"Nestlé"},{"name":"Unilever","emoji":"🧴","meta":["consumer goods","United Kingdom","1929"],"description":"The company was formed by the merger of the British soap maker Lever Brothers and the Dutch margarine firm Margarine Unie, which shared much the same vegetable-oil supply chains. Unilever now makes food, cosmetics and household products under dozens of brands.","fact":"One of Lever Brothers' first big products was Sunlight soap, sold in an individual wrapper — a rarity in the soap trade of the 19th century.","wiki_en":"Unilever"},{"name":"Procter & Gamble","emoji":"🧼","meta":["consumer goods","USA","1837"],"description":"The candlemaker William Procter and the soapmaker James Gamble joined their businesses after marrying two sisters and being advised by their father-in-law to cooperate rather than compete. P&G today makes dozens of well-known household and hygiene brands.","fact":"The company's 19th-century moon-and-stars logo drew rumours of satanic symbolism, and P&G had to drop it in the 1980s.","wiki_en":"Procter & Gamble"},{"name":"Johnson & Johnson","emoji":"🩹","meta":["pharmaceuticals","USA","1886"],"description":"The three Johnson brothers founded the company to make sterile dressings using the antiseptic methods that were then only beginning to reach surgery. Johnson & Johnson is now one of the world's largest makers of medical supplies, medicines and baby care products.","fact":"The Band-Aid, whose name became the everyday English word for a sticking plaster, was invented by an employee for his wife, who often cut herself in the kitchen.","wiki_en":"Johnson & Johnson"},{"name":"Pfizer","emoji":"💊","meta":["pharmaceuticals","USA","1849"],"description":"The company was founded by the cousins Charles Pfizer and Charles Erhart, immigrants from Germany, who started out making chemicals, among them an almond-flavoured remedy against intestinal worms. Pfizer is today one of the world's largest pharmaceutical groups.","fact":"The COVID-19 vaccine developed with the German firm BioNTech was among the first approved anywhere and made Pfizer known far beyond the drug industry.","wiki_en":"Pfizer"},{"name":"L'Oréal","emoji":"💄","meta":["cosmetics","France","1909"],"description":"The chemist Eugène Schueller developed one of the first safe hair dye formulas and began selling it to the hairdressers of Paris. That small business grew into the largest cosmetics company in the world, with dozens of brands under it.","fact":"The company long remained partly in the hands of the Bettencourt family: Liliane Bettencourt, the founder's daughter, was for years among the richest women in the world.","wiki_en":"L'Oréal"},{"name":"Red Bull","emoji":"🐂","meta":["beverages","Austria","1987"],"description":"The Austrian entrepreneur Dietrich Mateschitz tasted a tonic drink on a trip to Thailand and adapted it for the European market, creating the energy drink category itself. Red Bull is known today not only for the can but for its large-scale sponsorship of extreme sports.","fact":"The red bull on the logo nods to the Thai original, Krating Daeng, whose name means \"red bull\".","wiki_en":"Red Bull"},{"name":"Heineken","emoji":"🍺","meta":["beverages","Netherlands","1864"],"description":"The young entrepreneur Gerard Adriaan Heineken bought a brewery in Amsterdam meaning to fight the mass drinking of cheap gin by promoting beer as the more civilised option. Heineken is now one of the world's largest brewers, recognisable by its green bottle.","fact":"The star on the label goes back to an old European brewers' mark, hung on breweries long before the brand itself existed.","wiki_en":"Heineken N.V."},{"name":"Mars","emoji":"🍫","meta":["food","USA","1911"],"description":"Franklin Mars began boiling and selling sweets in his own kitchen, and real success came with the Milky Way bar; his son Forrest later added M&M's to the family business. Mars is now one of the world's largest makers of chocolate, chewing gum and pet food.","fact":"For all its size, Mars is still wholly owned by the founder's descendants and has never been listed on a stock exchange.","wiki_en":"Mars, Incorporated"},{"name":"Ferrero","emoji":"🌰","meta":["food","Italy","1946"],"description":"The Piedmontese confectioner Pietro Ferrero came up with a hazelnut spread as a cheaper answer to cocoa, which was scarce in post-war Italy. That recipe later became Nutella, and the company grew into one of the world's largest confectioners, with Kinder and Ferrero Rocher among its brands.","fact":"Kinder Surprise, the chocolate egg with a toy inside, appeared in the 1970s, but its sale was banned in the USA by rules against objects hidden in food.","wiki_en":"Ferrero SpA"},{"name":"Danone","emoji":"🥛","meta":["food","France","1919"],"description":"The entrepreneur Isaac Carasso began making yoghurt in Barcelona as a medicine sold in pharmacies, and named the company after his son Daniel's nickname. Danone is now one of the world's largest producers of dairy, baby food and bottled water.","fact":"In the USA the company traded for years as Dannon, because the French pronunciation of Danone did not sit easily on the American ear.","wiki_en":"Danone"},{"name":"Warner Bros.","emoji":"🎬","meta":["media","USA","1923"],"description":"The four Warner brothers founded a studio that was among the first to bet on sound, releasing \"The Jazz Singer\" in 1927 with synchronised speech and music. Warner Bros. is today one of the biggest Hollywood studios, home to the Harry Potter films and the DC superheroes.","fact":"The Looney Tunes cartoons, with Bugs Bunny and Daffy Duck, were created by the studio as an answer to the success of Disney's animation.","wiki_en":"Warner Bros."},{"name":"Marvel","emoji":"🕷️","meta":["media","USA","1939"],"description":"The comics publisher, which began as Timely Comics, made its name thanks to the writer Stan Lee and the artists who in the 1960s created Spider-Man, the Avengers and many other superheroes with human weaknesses. Marvel is also known now for its films, tied together in one cinematic universe.","fact":"Stan Lee traditionally made a cameo appearance in almost every film of the Marvel cinematic universe shot during his lifetime.","wiki_en":"Marvel Comics"},{"name":"Pixar","emoji":"💡","meta":["animation","USA","1986"],"description":"The company grew out of the computer division of George Lucas's studio, which Steve Jobs bought after leaving Apple. Pixar released the world's first fully computer-animated feature — \"Toy Story\" — and has been one of the leading animation studios ever since.","fact":"Luxo Jr., the hopping desk lamp that serves as the studio's mascot, starred in an early Pixar short film nominated for an Oscar.","wiki_en":"Pixar"},{"name":"Electronic Arts","emoji":"🎮","meta":["video games","USA","1982"],"description":"The company was founded by a former Apple employee, Trip Hawkins, who wanted to present game developers as rock stars, with their names on the covers just as musicians' names appear on records. Electronic Arts is now one of the largest game publishers, behind FIFA (today EA Sports FC) and The Sims.","fact":"Early EA releases were packaged like record albums, with portraits of the developers inside the gatefold — a rarity in the industry at the time.","wiki_en":"Electronic Arts"},{"name":"Activision Blizzard","emoji":"⚔️","meta":["video games","USA","2008"],"description":"The company was formed by the merger of Activision, one of the first independent video game developers in history, with Blizzard Entertainment, creator of World of Warcraft and Diablo. The combined company became one of the world's biggest game publishers and was bought by Microsoft in 2023.","fact":"Activision was founded in 1979 by programmers who had left Atari in protest at game creators not being credited on the packaging.","wiki_en":"Activision Blizzard"},{"name":"Valve","emoji":"🕹️","meta":["video games","USA","1996"],"description":"The company was founded by former Microsoft employees, who made their name first with the shooter Half-Life and then with Steam, the digital distribution platform that became the largest PC game shop in the world. Valve is still private, with an unusually flat structure and almost no bosses.","fact":"Valve has no middle managers as such: staff pick the projects they want to work on, literally rolling their desk over to the team they choose.","wiki_en":"Valve Corporation"},{"name":"Rockstar Games","emoji":"🚔","meta":["video games","USA","1998"],"description":"The studio made its name with the Grand Theft Auto series, whose open world effectively set the standard for a whole genre of video games. Rockstar Games also created the western Red Dead Redemption and other titles known for their detailed worlds and contested content.","fact":"The release of each new Grand Theft Auto is traditionally one of the biggest events in entertainment by first-day sales.","wiki_en":"Rockstar Games"},{"name":"Epic Games","emoji":"🏗️","meta":["video games","USA","1991"],"description":"The company was founded by the programmer Tim Sweeney, who started out selling his own games by mail, and won worldwide fame with the Unreal Engine and the battle royale Fortnite. Epic Games also runs its own digital games store, competing with Steam.","fact":"Fortnite Battle Royale began as an add-on to another, less successful game by the studio — the co-op survival Fortnite: Save the World.","wiki_en":"Epic Games"},{"name":"Telegram","emoji":"📨","meta":["technology","UAE","2013"],"description":"The messenger was built by the brothers Nikolai and Pavel Durov after they sold their Russian social network VKontakte, betting on speed, security and keeping chats in the cloud across every device. Telegram combines private chats, channels for publishing and a platform for bots.","fact":"Pavel Durov funded Telegram's development out of his own pocket for years, taking no venture money so as to keep control of the service.","wiki_en":"Telegram (software)"},{"name":"Yandex","emoji":"🧭","meta":["technology","Russia","1997"],"description":"The Russian company began with a search technology built for full-text search of Russian-language documents, long before the web service itself existed. Yandex is now Russia's largest internet company, running search, taxis, delivery and cloud services.","fact":"The name was coined from \"Yet Another iNDEXer\", and in Russian the opening I was swapped for the Cyrillic letter that means \"I\" — hence Yandex.","wiki_en":"Yandex"},{"name":"Sber","emoji":"💚","meta":["finance","Russia","1841"],"description":"The bank traces its history to the savings banks set up by a decree of Tsar Nicholas I, which makes it one of the oldest financial institutions in the country. Sber is today Russia's largest bank, building a broad ecosystem of digital services alongside its banking.","fact":"For a long time the bank was known as Sberbank; the rebranding to the shorter Sber came in the 21st century, along with its move into technology.","wiki_en":"Sberbank"},{"name":"Gazprom","emoji":"🔥","meta":["oil and gas","Russia","1989"],"description":"The company was created out of the Soviet ministry of the gas industry and inherited control of a large share of the country's fields and pipelines. Gazprom is one of the world's largest producers and exporters of natural gas.","fact":"Gazprom was long one of the few Russian companies whose shares traded widely on foreign exchanges as well as in Moscow.","wiki_en":"Gazprom"},{"name":"Rosneft","emoji":"⛏️","meta":["oil and gas","Russia","1993"],"description":"The company was assembled from the state oil-producing assets left over when the structure of the Soviet oil ministry broke up. Rosneft is today the largest oil producer in Russia and one of the biggest in the world by output.","fact":"In the 2000s Rosneft sharply increased its output by taking over assets that had previously belonged to Yukos.","wiki_en":"Rosneft"},{"name":"Lukoil","emoji":"🟥","meta":["oil and gas","Russia","1991"],"description":"The name is made from the first letters of three West Siberian oil towns — Langepas, Uray and Kogalym — where its first production sites stood. Lukoil is one of Russia's largest private oil companies, with a wide network of filling stations abroad.","fact":"Lukoil is one of the few large Russian oil companies not under direct state control.","wiki_en":"Lukoil"},{"name":"VK","emoji":"💬","meta":["technology","Russia","2006"],"description":"The company began as the social network VKontakte, built by Pavel Durov along the lines of America's Facebook, and grew over time into a large technology holding with email, video hosting and games. The group now trades under the single VK brand.","fact":"Pavel Durov, who founded VKontakte, later left both the company and Russia and created the Telegram messenger, which has no connection to VK.","wiki_en":"VK (company)"},{"name":"T-Bank","emoji":"💛","meta":["finance","Russia","2006"],"description":"The bank was founded by the entrepreneur Oleg Tinkov as one of Russia's first entirely branchless banks, serving customers over the internet and through couriers instead of offices. After a rebranding, the bank once known as Tinkoff became T-Bank.","fact":"At first the bank dealt only in credit cards, mailing offers to customers, and only later widened its range into a full online bank.","wiki_en":"Tinkoff Bank"},{"name":"Ozon","emoji":"🔵","meta":["e-commerce","Russia","1998"],"description":"The company started as an online bookshop modelled on Amazon and grew into one of Russia's largest marketplaces, carrying goods from thousands of sellers. The name points to ozone, a byword for freshness and cleanliness.","fact":"Ozon began as a project of the St Petersburg IT company Reksoft and only later became a large business in its own right.","wiki_en":"Ozon (company)"},{"name":"Wildberries","emoji":"🍇","meta":["e-commerce","Russia","2004"],"description":"Tatyana Bakalchuk started an online clothes shop while on maternity leave, selling catalogue clothing from Germany to friends and colleagues. Wildberries is now one of the largest marketplaces in Russia and its neighbours, with a vast range of goods.","fact":"The marketplace that grew out of that home business has repeatedly put Tatyana Bakalchuk at the top of the lists of Russia's richest women.","wiki_en":"Wildberries"},{"name":"Aeroflot","emoji":"🛫","meta":["airlines","Russia","1923"],"description":"The airline traces its history to the Soviet society Dobrolet, set up to develop civil aviation in the young Soviet republic, and for decades it was the only airline in the USSR. Aeroflot is today the largest carrier in Russia.","fact":"Aeroflot's logo, a winged hammer and sickle introduced in Soviet times, is still used by the company in modified form.","wiki_en":"Aeroflot"},{"name":"AvtoVAZ","emoji":"🚘","meta":["automotive","Russia","1966"],"description":"The plant in Tolyatti was built with help from Italy's Fiat, and its first mass model was the VAZ-2101, the Zhiguli — a version of the Fiat 124 adapted for Soviet roads. AvtoVAZ now builds cars under the Lada name and remains Russia's largest carmaker.","fact":"Export models were sold as Lada rather than Zhiguli, because the original name sounded awkward in some European languages.","wiki_en":"AvtoVAZ"},{"name":"Kaspersky Lab","emoji":"🛡️","meta":["technology","Russia","1997"],"description":"The company was founded by the programmer Eugene Kaspersky together with colleagues who had been building antivirus technology back in Soviet times. Kaspersky Lab is today one of the world's better-known developers of antivirus and security software.","fact":"Kaspersky started writing the first antivirus that led to the company after finding a virus on his own work computer in the late 1980s.","wiki_en":"Kaspersky Lab"},{"name":"Russian Railways","emoji":"🚂","meta":["rail transport","Russia","2003"],"description":"The company was created by the reform of the Soviet and Russian railway system, bringing an enormous network of track and rolling stock under one management. Russian Railways is one of the world's largest rail systems by length of track.","fact":"The network it runs was begun in the 19th century, with the Tsarskoye Selo line of 1837 and the railway that linked St Petersburg to Moscow.","wiki_en":"Russian Railways"},{"name":"Nornickel","emoji":"🪨","meta":["mining","Russia","1935"],"description":"The complex grew up around deposits beyond the Arctic Circle, where large reserves of nickel, copper and platinum group metals were found. Nornickel is today one of the world's largest producers of nickel and palladium.","fact":"Norilsk, the city where the company's main plants stand, was largely built by Gulag prisoners in the harsh conditions of the Far North.","wiki_en":"Norilsk Nickel"},{"name":"Rosatom","emoji":"☢️","meta":["nuclear energy","Russia","2007"],"description":"The state corporation brought the enterprises of the Soviet nuclear industry under one roof — from uranium mining to building power stations and the nuclear icebreaker fleet. Rosatom is still among the world leaders by number of reactor units under construction abroad.","fact":"Rosatom operates the world's only working fleet of nuclear icebreakers, which keep the Northern Sea Route navigable.","wiki_en":"Rosatom"},{"name":"Kalashnikov Concern","emoji":"🔫","meta":["defence industry","Russia","2013"],"description":"The group brought together the production lines of the Izhevsk machine works, where the Kalashnikov rifles designed by Mikhail Kalashnikov have been built since the middle of the 20th century. The company now makes not only small arms but other high-technology products.","fact":"The Kalashnikov rifle, designed in the 1940s, became the most widely produced firearm in history and appears on the coats of arms or flags of several countries.","wiki_en":"Kalashnikov Concern"},{"name":"Colgate","emoji":"🪥","meta":["personal care","USA","1806"],"description":"An American brand of toothpaste and toothbrushes, among the most recognisable oral care products in the world. It belongs to Colgate-Palmolive and is sold in dozens of countries; for many people the name is almost a synonym for toothpaste itself.","fact":"The company started out making soap and candles, and brought out toothpaste in tubes only decades after it was founded.","wiki_en":"Colgate (toothpaste)"},{"name":"Gillette","emoji":"🪒","meta":["shaving products","USA","1901"],"description":"An American brand of razors and shaving products, one of the pioneers of the replaceable blade. It belongs to Procter & Gamble and was promoted for decades with the slogan \"The Best a Man Can Get\". Its products are sold in almost every country in the world.","fact":"The founder, King Camp Gillette, came up with the replaceable-blade razor after being taken with the idea of disposable goods.","wiki_en":"Gillette"},{"name":"Pampers","emoji":"👶","meta":["baby products","USA","1961"],"description":"A brand of disposable nappies owned by Procter & Gamble, one of the most recognisable baby products in the world. The name was chosen to suggest care and comfort for the child. The nappies come in ranges for different ages and are sold worldwide.","fact":"Work on the nappies was started by a Procter & Gamble engineer, Victor Mills, who was tired of washing cloth nappies for his grandchildren.","wiki_en":"Pampers"},{"name":"Dove","emoji":"🕊️","meta":["cosmetics and care","USA","1957"],"description":"A brand of soap, shower products and skincare owned by Unilever. It began with a beauty bar containing moisturising cream, which became the mark's central selling point. Dove now makes a wide range of cosmetics and is known for its \"Real Beauty\" campaign about natural looks.","fact":"The brand's first advertisements promised that Dove would not dry the skin the way ordinary soap does — its whole reputation was built on that.","wiki_en":"Dove (toiletries)"},{"name":"Nivea","emoji":"🧴","meta":["cosmetics","Germany","1911"],"description":"A German skincare brand, one of the oldest and most recognisable in the world. It started with a cream in a blue metal tin that became the mark's signature for decades to come. Nivea now makes creams, lotions, deodorants and shaving products.","fact":"The name comes from the Latin word nivea — \"snow-white\".","wiki_en":"Nivea"},{"name":"Old Spice","emoji":"🧴","meta":["men's grooming","USA","1937"],"description":"An American brand of men's deodorants, colognes and shaving products, with a sailing ship for a logo. For a long time it traded on nautical themes and an image of manliness. Owned by Procter & Gamble, it grew especially popular after a viral ad campaign in the early 2010s.","fact":"Old Spice originally made perfume for women, and only later turned into an entirely men's line.","wiki_en":"Old Spice"},{"name":"Kellogg's","emoji":"🥣","meta":["food","USA","1906"],"description":"An American company, one of the world's largest makers of breakfast cereals. It began with corn flakes, invented by the Kellogg brothers as part of a healthy eating regime. Kellogg's now sells dozens of brands of cereal bars and ready-made breakfasts around the world.","fact":"Corn flakes came about almost by accident, during experiments with boiled wheat for the patients of a sanatorium.","wiki_en":"Kellogg's"},{"name":"Lipton","emoji":"🍵","meta":["tea and drinks","United Kingdom","1890"],"description":"A British brand of black and herbal tea, among the best-selling in the world. It was founded by the Scottish entrepreneur Thomas Lipton, who began as a grocer and later took on tea plantations of his own. Tea under this label is sold in more than a hundred countries.","fact":"The founder bought tea plantations in Ceylon so that he could sell tea straight to customers, cutting out the middlemen.","wiki_en":"Lipton","wiki_ru":"Lipton"},{"name":"Nescafé","emoji":"☕","meta":["beverages","Switzerland","1938"],"description":"Nestlé's instant coffee brand, one of the first of its kind and the best-selling in the world. It was created at the request of Brazil, which was looking for a way to use up surplus coffee beans. Nescafé now comes in dozens of forms, from classic powder to capsules.","fact":"It took Nestlé's chemists about seven years of experiments to work out the formula for instant coffee.","wiki_en":"Nescafé"},{"name":"Monster Energy","emoji":"⚡","meta":["energy drinks","USA","2002"],"description":"An American energy drink brand whose logo is a set of claw marks in the shape of an M. It quickly became one of the market leaders, competing with Red Bull, and sponsors extreme sports and esports heavily. It comes in many flavours and can sizes.","fact":"The company behind the drink started out making Hansen's natural juices and only later renamed itself Monster Beverage.","wiki_en":"Monster Energy"},{"name":"Fanta","emoji":"🍊","meta":["beverages","Germany","1940"],"description":"A fizzy fruit drink created by Coca-Cola's German arm during the Second World War, when the ingredients for the original cola ran short. It now comes in dozens of flavours, of which orange is the best known, and belongs to The Coca-Cola Company.","fact":"The name came out of a competition among staff — it derives from the German word \"Fantasie\", imagination.","wiki_en":"Fanta"},{"name":"Sprite","emoji":"🍋","meta":["beverages","USA","1961"],"description":"A caffeine-free lemon-lime fizzy drink made by The Coca-Cola Company. It was created as an answer to the rising popularity of 7 Up and quickly became one of the best-selling soft drinks in the world. It is clear in colour, with a recognisable green label.","fact":"The name Sprite was first used for an elf character in Coca-Cola advertising back in the 1940s.","wiki_en":"Sprite (drink)"},{"name":"Dr Pepper","emoji":"🥤","meta":["beverages","USA","1885"],"description":"An American fizzy drink with a distinctive taste that blends more than twenty spice and fruit notes. It is reckoned one of the oldest soft drink brands in the USA, older than both Coca-Cola and Pepsi. It is made today by Keurig Dr Pepper.","fact":"The exact recipe behind the Dr Pepper flavour is kept secret and has been argued over by tasters for more than a century.","wiki_en":"Dr Pepper"},{"name":"Oreo","emoji":"🍪","meta":["confectionery","USA","1912"],"description":"An American biscuit of two chocolate wafers with a cream filling, the best-selling biscuit in the world. Made by Mondelez International, it comes with its own ritual — twist, lick, dunk in a glass of milk — and exists in dozens of flavours.","fact":"The modern pattern stamped on the biscuit, with its four-leaf design and the word Oreo in a circle, appeared only in 1952.","wiki_en":"Oreo"},{"name":"Milka","emoji":"🍫","meta":["confectionery","Switzerland","1901"],"description":"A Swiss milk chocolate brand with its trademark lilac wrapper and purple cow logo. It now belongs to Mondelez International and is sold across much of Europe. The name is a contraction of the German words \"Milch\" and \"Kakao\".","fact":"The lilac of the wrapper is registered as a protected trademark — no other chocolate maker may use it.","wiki_en":"Milka"},{"name":"Snickers","emoji":"🍫","meta":["confectionery","USA","1930"],"description":"A chocolate bar with caramel, peanuts and nougat, one of the best-selling sweets in the world. Made by Mars, it is named after the founding family's favourite horse, and is known for the advertising line about hunger making you \"not you\".","fact":"The bar was named after the horse Snickers, which died shortly before the product reached the market.","wiki_en":"Snickers"},{"name":"M&M's","emoji":"🍬","meta":["confectionery","USA","1941"],"description":"Milk chocolate buttons in a coloured sugar shell, made by Mars. They were originally created for soldiers, so that the chocolate would not melt in the hand or in hot climates. Today they are recognisable from the bright talking-button characters in their advertising.","fact":"The \"M&M's\" letters are printed on each sweet with edible ink, and the name stands for the makers, Mars and Murrie.","wiki_en":"M&M's"},{"name":"Skittles","emoji":"🌈","meta":["confectionery","United Kingdom","1974"],"description":"Colourful fruit-flavoured sweets in a sugar shell, owned by Mars. They are known for the slogan \"Taste the Rainbow\" and for adverts with surreal humour, and are sold worldwide in several flavour ranges.","fact":"Skittles were at first sold only in Europe and reached the American market several years later.","wiki_en":"Skittles (confectionery)"},{"name":"Twix","emoji":"🍫","meta":["confectionery","United Kingdom","1967"],"description":"A chocolate bar made of two crisp biscuit fingers covered in caramel and chocolate. Made by Mars, it comes in a pack of two separate bars, and is known for the advertising story about the \"left\" and \"right\" Twix.","fact":"In continental Europe the bar was sold for decades as Raider and was renamed Twix only in the 1990s.","wiki_en":"Twix"},{"name":"KitKat","emoji":"🍫","meta":["confectionery","United Kingdom","1935"],"description":"A chocolate bar of crisp wafer fingers covered in milk chocolate and split into snap-off pieces. Rights to the brand in different parts of the world belong to Nestlé and to Hershey. It is known for the slogan \"Have a break, have a Kit Kat\".","fact":"Japan sells dozens of unusual KitKat flavours, including wasabi and matcha green tea.","wiki_en":"Kit Kat"},{"name":"Lay's","emoji":"🥔","meta":["snacks","USA","1932"],"description":"An American brand of potato crisps owned by PepsiCo, among the best-selling in the world. It started with a travelling salesman, Herman Lay, selling crisps out of the boot of his car. Today it comes in dozens of flavours tailored to different countries.","fact":"In some countries the brand is sold under other names, such as Walkers in the United Kingdom.","wiki_en":"Lay's"},{"name":"Pringles","emoji":"🥔","meta":["snacks","USA","1968"],"description":"An American crisp brand with a regular saddle shape, stacked inside a recognisable cylindrical tube. Unlike ordinary crisps, they are made from pressed potato dough. The brand now belongs to the company Kellanova.","fact":"The scientist who devised the shape of the crisp asked in his will for part of his ashes to be buried in a Pringles tube.","wiki_en":"Pringles"},{"name":"Doritos","emoji":"🌽","meta":["snacks","USA","1964"],"description":"An American brand of triangular corn chips with bold seasonings, owned by PepsiCo. They were dreamt up by staff at Disneyland who were selling leftover tortillas from the restaurant menu. Today they are among the best-selling snacks in the USA.","fact":"The original idea grew out of a wish not to throw away stale corn tortillas at the park's restaurant.","wiki_en":"Doritos"},{"name":"Heinz","emoji":"🍅","meta":["food","USA","1869"],"description":"An American company known the world over above all for its ketchup in that recognisable glass bottle. Founded by Henry Heinz, it came to make hundreds of products, from sauces to tinned goods. The slogan \"57 varieties\" has long been part of the brand's look.","fact":"The number 57 on the ketchup label never matched the real count of the company's products — the founder simply liked it.","wiki_en":"H. J. Heinz Company"},{"name":"Lindt","emoji":"🍫","meta":["confectionery","Switzerland","1845"],"description":"A Swiss company known for its premium chocolate and its gold chocolate bunnies at Easter. It was one of the pioneers of conching, the process that made chocolate smoother and left it melting on the tongue. Its products are sold in dozens of countries.","fact":"Conching, which changed the texture of chocolate, was invented by the Swiss chocolatier Rodolphe Lindt, whose name the brand carries.","wiki_en":"Lindt & Sprüngli"},{"name":"Ben & Jerry's","emoji":"🍦","meta":["ice cream","USA","1978"],"description":"An American ice cream brand founded by two childhood friends in a converted petrol station in Vermont. It is known for unusual flavours and for speaking out openly on social and political questions. It is now part of Unilever.","fact":"The founders took a cheap correspondence course in ice cream making before opening a business of their own.","wiki_en":"Ben & Jerry's"},{"name":"Dunkin'","emoji":"🍩","meta":["food service","USA","1950"],"description":"An American chain of coffee and doughnut shops, among the largest in the world by number of outlets. It began with a single doughnut shop in Massachusetts. Formerly Dunkin' Donuts, it shortened its name in 2019 to put the accent on coffee.","fact":"The company dropped the word \"Donuts\" from its name to underline the growing share of coffee and drinks in its sales.","wiki_en":"Dunkin'"},{"name":"Domino's Pizza","emoji":"🍕","meta":["food service","USA","1960"],"description":"An American pizza chain, one of the world's largest in home delivery. For years it traded on a promise to deliver an order within 30 minutes. It pushes hard on online ordering and on tracking deliveries in real time.","fact":"The three dots on the company's logo originally stood for the founders' first three pizzerias.","wiki_en":"Domino's Pizza"},{"name":"Pizza Hut","emoji":"🍕","meta":["food service","USA","1958"],"description":"An American pizza chain with the recognisable red roof over its restaurant buildings. One of the oldest big pizza chains, it works both as sit-down restaurants and as delivery. It was founded by two student brothers on borrowed money.","fact":"The name came about almost by accident — the sign on the building had room for only nine letters, which settled on the word \"Hut\".","wiki_en":"Pizza Hut"},{"name":"Wendy's","emoji":"🍔","meta":["food service","USA","1969"],"description":"An American fast-food chain known for its square burgers and the red-haired girl on its logo. It is named after the daughter of the founder Dave Thomas, nicknamed Wendy. It is one of the largest burger chains in the world.","fact":"The square patties in Wendy's burgers are meant to show that the meat is not trimmed down to fit a round bun.","wiki_en":"Wendy's"},{"name":"Taco Bell","emoji":"🌮","meta":["food service","USA","1962"],"description":"An American fast-food chain serving Mexican food, one of the largest in the world. It is known for tacos, burritos and hot sauces, and for unusual experiments on its menu. It belongs to the company Yum! Brands.","fact":"The founder, Glen Bell, first ran a hot dog stand and only later switched to Mexican food.","wiki_en":"Taco Bell"},{"name":"Costco","emoji":"🛒","meta":["retail","USA","1983"],"description":"An American chain of warehouse stores that runs on paid membership and sells goods in large bulk packs. It is one of the world's biggest retailers by revenue, known for low prices and for the famous hot dog and drink whose price has stayed the same for decades.","fact":"The price of the hot dog and drink in Costco's food court has not changed since 1985.","wiki_en":"Costco"},{"name":"Target","emoji":"🎯","meta":["retail","USA","1962"],"description":"An American chain of general stores with its recognisable red bullseye logo. It sells clothes, homeware, electronics and food, positioning itself as a more stylish alternative to the discount chains. It is one of the largest retailers in the USA.","fact":"The bullseye logo came from a play on words: a target is the thing you aim at.","wiki_en":"Target Corporation"},{"name":"Home Depot","emoji":"🔨","meta":["retail","USA","1978"],"description":"An American chain of stores for home, repair and building supplies, the largest of its kind in the world. It stands out for a vast range and warehouse-style shop floors. It leans heavily on customers doing the work themselves.","fact":"The chain's first stores opened in Atlanta, in the huge empty buildings of a retail chain that had gone out of business.","wiki_en":"The Home Depot"},{"name":"Best Buy","emoji":"🔌","meta":["retail","USA","1966"],"description":"An American chain of consumer electronics stores, one of the largest in the category. It began as an audio equipment shop under a different name and changed it after a run of successful sales. It sells devices, gadgets and services to install and repair them.","fact":"The company was originally called Sound of Music and dealt only in audio equipment.","wiki_en":"Best Buy"},{"name":"7-Eleven","emoji":"🏪","meta":["retail","USA","1927"],"description":"An international chain of round-the-clock corner shops, one of the largest in the world by number of outlets. The name reflects its original hours, 7 in the morning to 11 at night, though most shops now stay open all day. It is especially widespread in Japan and elsewhere in Asia.","fact":"Although the chain is American in origin, its parent company today belongs to a Japanese corporation.","wiki_en":"7-Eleven"},{"name":"FedEx","emoji":"📦","meta":["logistics","USA","1971"],"description":"An American express delivery and logistics company, one of the pioneers of overnight parcel delivery worldwide. The name is short for Federal Express, which it originally traded as. It runs a large fleet of its own aircraft for international freight.","fact":"The company's logo hides a white arrow between the letters E and x, standing for speed and precision.","wiki_en":"FedEx"},{"name":"UPS","emoji":"📦","meta":["logistics","USA","1907"],"description":"An American parcel delivery and logistics company, one of the oldest and largest in the industry. It began as a small messenger service in Seattle. It is recognisable by the brown of its trucks and its couriers' uniforms.","fact":"Brown was chosen for the uniforms and the vehicles back in the early 20th century, because it hid the dirt of the road.","wiki_en":"United Parcel Service"},{"name":"DHL","emoji":"📦","meta":["logistics","USA, now Germany","1969"],"description":"An international express delivery and logistics company, founded in San Francisco and today part of Germany's Deutsche Post. The name is made from the initials of its three founders — Dalsey, Hillblom and Lynn. It operates in practically every country in the world.","fact":"The company started by flying documents between San Francisco and Honolulu, beating the cargo ships.","wiki_en":"DHL"},{"name":"American Express","emoji":"💳","meta":["finance","USA","1850"],"description":"An American financial company known above all for its premium payment cards of the same name. It began as a courier service carrying valuables, not as a financial institution. It now issues cards and travellers' cheques and provides banking services.","fact":"The company issued a plastic payment card only years after the success of its paper travellers' cheques.","wiki_en":"American Express"},{"name":"Bank of America","emoji":"🏦","meta":["banking","USA","1904"],"description":"One of the largest banks in the USA, offering retail, corporate and investment services. It grew out of a small San Francisco bank aimed at immigrants whom other banks turned away. It now serves tens of millions of customers across the country.","fact":"The bank's founder was among the first in the country to lend to ordinary workers and small traders, not only to well-off clients.","wiki_en":"Bank of America"},{"name":"HSBC","emoji":"🏦","meta":["banking","United Kingdom","1865"],"description":"One of the largest international banks in the world, with its head office in London. The name is an abbreviation of Hongkong and Shanghai Banking Corporation, a nod to the company's Asian roots. It serves customers in dozens of countries on several continents.","fact":"The bank was set up in the first place to finance trade between Europe and Asia through Hong Kong.","wiki_en":"HSBC"},{"name":"Levi's","emoji":"👖","meta":["clothing","USA","1853"],"description":"An American clothing brand that invented the classic jeans, with rivets on the pockets for strength. It was founded by a Bavarian immigrant, Levi Strauss, during the California gold rush. The 501 is reckoned one of the most recognisable garments in the history of fashion.","fact":"The rivets on the pockets were patented not by Strauss himself but by the tailor Jacob Davis, who then became his partner.","wiki_en":"Levi Strauss & Co."},{"name":"Calvin Klein","emoji":"👔","meta":["clothing","USA","1968"],"description":"An American fashion house known for its minimalist style, its underwear and its perfumes with the recognisable CK logo. It was founded by the designer Calvin Klein, who started out making coats in a small studio. It became one of the emblems of American fashion in the 1980s and 1990s.","fact":"The brand's advertising campaigns with young celebrities in the 1980s repeatedly caused public argument and filled the press.","wiki_en":"Calvin Klein (fashion house)","wiki_ru":"Calvin Klein"},{"name":"Tommy Hilfiger","emoji":"👕","meta":["clothing","USA","1985"],"description":"An American fashion brand known for preppy casual clothes and the blue, white and red flag on its logo. The founder started out with a small jeans shop in a provincial town. The label is now sold worldwide and belongs to the PVH group.","fact":"The founder brought out his first own collection after his first clothes shop had gone bust.","wiki_en":"Tommy Hilfiger (company)","wiki_ru":"Tommy Hilfiger"},{"name":"Ralph Lauren","emoji":"🐎","meta":["clothing","USA","1967"],"description":"An American fashion house known for its elegant style and the logo of a polo player on horseback. The founder, Ralph Lauren, started out selling ties of his own design. The brand is bound up with an image of American gentry and with classic cuts.","fact":"The founder's real surname was Lifshitz; he changed it to Lauren while still a young man.","wiki_en":"Ralph Lauren Corporation"},{"name":"Victoria's Secret","emoji":"👙","meta":["clothing","USA","1977"],"description":"An American brand of women's lingerie, known for its annual fashion shows with supermodel \"angels\". Its name and the fitting-out of its shops were originally styled after the Victorian era. It is one of the most recognisable lingerie brands in the world.","fact":"The first shop was opened by an entrepreneur who felt awkward buying lingerie for his wife in ordinary stores.","wiki_en":"Victoria's Secret"},{"name":"Under Armour","emoji":"🏋️","meta":["sportswear","USA","1996"],"description":"An American sportswear and kit brand that started with compression shirts designed to wick sweat away from the body. The founder, Kevin Plank, hit on the idea while captain of his university football team. It now makes shoes, clothing and accessories for sport.","fact":"The founder sewed the first shirts himself from synthetic fabric bought in a fabric shop — it drew moisture away better than cotton.","wiki_en":"Under Armour"},{"name":"New Balance","emoji":"👟","meta":["athletic footwear","USA","1906"],"description":"An American athletic footwear company, one of the few large trainer brands that still makes part of its output in the USA and the United Kingdom. It began by making orthopaedic insoles and arch supports. It is known for its wide range of sizes and widths.","fact":"The name points to the idea of a \"new balance\" — the balance of the foot that its first orthopaedic products improved.","wiki_en":"New Balance"},{"name":"Reebok","emoji":"👟","meta":["athletic footwear","United Kingdom","1958"],"description":"A British brand of sports shoes and clothing that grew out of a small running-shoe factory from the early 20th century. Its name comes from the rhebok, an African antelope that stands for speed. It now makes footwear and clothing for fitness, running and CrossFit.","fact":"The company became known in the USA for releasing some of the first trainers made specially for aerobics.","wiki_en":"Reebok","wiki_ru":"Reebok"},{"name":"Converse","emoji":"👟","meta":["footwear","USA","1908"],"description":"An American shoe company that created the legendary Chuck Taylor All Star, which became a symbol of 20th-century youth culture. It originally made rubber footwear for bad weather rather than for sport. It is now part of Nike.","fact":"The All Star was named after the basketball player Chuck Taylor, who promoted the shoes and helped improve their design.","wiki_en":"Converse (shoe company)"},{"name":"Vans","emoji":"🛹","meta":["footwear","USA","1966"],"description":"An American shoe and clothing brand closely tied to skateboarding and surf culture. It began by selling shoes straight from the workshop on the day they were made, with no middlemen. Its checkerboard model became one of the emblems of street fashion.","fact":"The company's first shop sold shoes on the very day they were stitched — customers could even choose the fabric for their pair.","wiki_en":"Vans"},{"name":"Prada","emoji":"👜","meta":["fashion and luxury","Italy","1913"],"description":"An Italian fashion house known for its leather goods, its bags and the minimalist design of its clothes. It began as a shop for leather goods and luggage in Milan. It now counts among the most influential luxury brands in the world.","fact":"Worldwide fame came to the brand through the founder's granddaughter Miuccia Prada, who took over the company and turned it into a fashion house.","wiki_en":"Prada"},{"name":"Versace","emoji":"👗","meta":["fashion and luxury","Italy","1978"],"description":"An Italian fashion house known for bold prints, daring cuts and a logo in the shape of a Medusa head. It was founded by the designer Gianni Versace in Milan. After his death the company was taken over by his sister Donatella Versace.","fact":"The Medusa head was chosen as a symbol of a beauty able to captivate and enchant for ever.","wiki_en":"Versace"},{"name":"Armani","emoji":"🕴️","meta":["fashion and luxury","Italy","1975"],"description":"An Italian fashion house known for its sharp suits and the minimalist elegance of its silhouettes. It was founded by the designer Giorgio Armani, who had earlier worked as a window dresser and a buyer. The brand became a byword for understated luxury in men's and women's fashion.","fact":"Before becoming a designer, Giorgio Armani spent several years studying medicine.","wiki_en":"Armani"},{"name":"Dior","emoji":"👗","meta":["fashion and luxury","France","1946"],"description":"A French fashion house founded by the couturier Christian Dior, which made its name with the New Look — a tiny waist and full skirts. It is one of the world's leading houses of haute couture and perfume, and is now part of the LVMH group.","fact":"The New Look collection caused a sensation after the war, when women were starved of luxurious fabric after years of austerity.","wiki_en":"Dior"},{"name":"Burberry","emoji":"🧥","meta":["fashion and luxury","United Kingdom","1856"],"description":"A British fashion house known above all for its trench coats and its signature check. It began by making a tough waterproof cloth for the clothing of travellers and soldiers. The Burberry check became one of the most recognisable patterns in fashion.","fact":"Gabardine, the cloth invented by the company's founder, was used in the clothing of polar expeditions in the early 20th century.","wiki_en":"Burberry"},{"name":"Tiffany & Co.","emoji":"💎","meta":["jewellery","USA","1837"],"description":"An American jeweller known for its jewellery and its turquoise packaging, a colour that now carries the company's own name. It began as a shop for stationery and fancy goods in New York. It is now part of the French conglomerate LVMH.","fact":"The turquoise of the boxes is so recognisable that the shade is called Tiffany Blue.","wiki_en":"Tiffany & Co."},{"name":"Cartier","emoji":"💍","meta":["jewellery","France","1847"],"description":"A French jeweller known for its jewellery and watches, once called the jeweller of kings and the king of jewellers. It made pieces for the royal houses of Europe. It now belongs to the Swiss holding company Richemont.","fact":"Cartier devised the wristwatch for an aviator who found it awkward to pull out a pocket watch in flight.","wiki_en":"Cartier (jeweler)"},{"name":"Dyson","emoji":"🌀","meta":["home appliances","United Kingdom","1991"],"description":"A British company known for bagless household appliances built on cyclone technology. The founder, James Dyson, got a working vacuum cleaner prototype only after several thousand failed attempts. It now also makes hairdryers, air purifiers and fans.","fact":"Before he found an investor, the founder had built more than five thousand prototypes of his vacuum cleaner.","wiki_en":"Dyson (company)"},{"name":"GoPro","emoji":"📷","meta":["electronics","USA","2002"],"description":"An American company that makes compact action cameras for filming during sport and the outdoors. The founder hit on the idea while trying to film his own surfing. The cameras became popular with travellers, athletes and video bloggers.","fact":"The founder assembled the first camera himself, using an old film camera and a strap to fasten it to his wrist while surfing.","wiki_en":"GoPro","wiki_ru":"GoPro"},{"name":"Bose","emoji":"🔊","meta":["audio","USA","1964"],"description":"An American company specialising in loudspeakers and headphones with active noise cancellation. It was founded by Amar Bose, a professor at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology. It is known for its sound quality and a research-minded approach to design.","fact":"For a long time the company stayed in the founder's private hands and never listed its shares on an exchange.","wiki_en":"Bose Corporation","wiki_ru":"Bose Corporation"},{"name":"Harley-Davidson","emoji":"🏍️","meta":["motorcycles","USA","1903"],"description":"An American company that builds heavy motorcycles with an unmistakable low, rumbling engine note. It began with four enthusiastic friends working in a small shed. It became a symbol of freedom and of American biker culture.","fact":"The company even tried to trademark the sound of its engine, but in the end gave the idea up.","wiki_en":"Harley-Davidson"},{"name":"Jeep","emoji":"🚙","meta":["cars","USA","1941"],"description":"An American marque of off-road vehicles, descended from an army runabout of the Second World War. In many languages the name became the everyday word for an off-roader of any sort. It is now part of the Stellantis group.","fact":"The exact origin of the name Jeep is still argued over — one version ties it to the abbreviation GP, for general purpose.","wiki_en":"Jeep"},{"name":"Chevrolet","emoji":"🚗","meta":["cars","USA","1911"],"description":"An American car marque, one of the oldest and largest brands in the General Motors group. It is named after the Swiss racing driver and engineer Louis Chevrolet, one of the company's founders. It makes both mass-market models and sports cars such as the Corvette.","fact":"Louis Chevrolet fell out with his partners and left the company within a few years, never growing rich on the brand that carried his name.","wiki_en":"Chevrolet"},{"name":"Lamborghini","emoji":"🐂","meta":["cars","Italy","1963"],"description":"An Italian maker of sports cars, known for striking design and a bull on its badge. The founder, Ferruccio Lamborghini, started out building tractors and turned to cars after falling out with Enzo Ferrari. It is now part of the Volkswagen group.","fact":"Legend has it that the founder decided to build sports cars after Enzo Ferrari brushed off his complaints about the clutch on Ferrari's cars.","wiki_en":"Lamborghini"},{"name":"Bentley","emoji":"🚘","meta":["cars","United Kingdom","1919"],"description":"A British marque of luxury cars, combining a sporting character with the highest level of comfort and interior finish. It was founded by the racing driver Walter Owen Bentley. It is now part of the Volkswagen group.","fact":"Bentleys won the famous Le Mans 24 Hours endurance race several times in the 1920s.","wiki_en":"Bentley","wiki_ru":"Bentley Motors"},{"name":"Rolls-Royce","emoji":"👑","meta":["cars","United Kingdom","1904"],"description":"A British marque of luxury cars, a byword for opulence and flawless build quality. It was founded by the engineer Henry Royce and the businessman Charles Rolls. The Spirit of Ecstasy figure on the bonnet is one of the most recognisable symbols in the car industry.","fact":"Apart from cars, a separate and independent Rolls-Royce company develops aircraft engines.","wiki_en":"Rolls-Royce Motor Cars","wiki_ru":"Rolls-Royce Motor Cars"},{"name":"Volvo","emoji":"🚗","meta":["cars","Sweden","1927"],"description":"A Swedish car marque known above all for its high standards of safety. It was the company's engineers who invented the three-point seatbelt and then gave the patent away free to the whole industry. The car arm now belongs to China's Geely.","fact":"The company opened its three-point seatbelt patent to every carmaker free of charge, for the sake of saving lives.","wiki_en":"Volvo Cars"},{"name":"Kia","emoji":"🚗","meta":["cars","South Korea","1944"],"description":"A South Korean car company, one of the oldest in the country, which began by making bicycle parts and components. It is now in the same group as Hyundai and sells mass-market models worldwide. In recent years it has become known for bold modern design.","fact":"The name Kia is made from characters that can be read as \"rising out of Asia\".","wiki_en":"Kia Corporation"},{"name":"Magnit","emoji":"🛒","meta":["retail","Russia","1994"],"description":"One of Russia's largest retail chains, with shops in several formats — from corner stores to hypermarkets and drugstores. It began as a wholesale business in household chemicals and perfume in Krasnodar. It now operates in thousands of towns and villages.","fact":"The chain's founder, Sergei Galitsky, later put his own money into building a football stadium and club in Krasnodar.","wiki_en":"Magnit"},{"name":"Pyaterochka","emoji":"🛒","meta":["retail","Russia","1998"],"description":"A Russian chain of convenience stores, one of the largest in the country by number of outlets. It is part of X5 Group along with the Perekrestok and Chizhik chains. It is built around everyday shopping within walking distance of home.","fact":"Pyaterochka has around twenty thousand shops in Russia — the country's largest retail chain by number of outlets.","wiki_en":"Pyaterochka","wiki_ru":"Пятёрочка"},{"name":"Perekrestok","emoji":"🛒","meta":["retail","Russia","1995"],"description":"A Russian supermarket chain aimed at a wider and better range than the corner-shop format offers. It was one of the first modern supermarkets in post-Soviet Russia, and it is part of X5 Group.","fact":"The chain grew out of a project of Alfa Group and in the mid-2000s merged with Pyaterochka into the single company X5.","wiki_en":"Perekrestok (supermarket chain)","wiki_ru":"Перекрёсток (сеть магазинов)"},{"name":"Lenta","emoji":"🛒","meta":["retail","Russia","1993"],"description":"A Russian chain of hypermarkets and supermarkets that began as a cash-and-carry store in St Petersburg. It is one of the country's largest retailers by floor space. In recent years it has also been pushing into smaller store formats.","fact":"The chain grew out of a St Petersburg cash-and-carry shop where customers took goods straight off the warehouse shelves.","wiki_en":"Lenta (retail)","wiki_ru":"Лента (сеть магазинов)"},{"name":"M.Video","emoji":"📺","meta":["retail","Russia","1993"],"description":"A Russian chain of home appliance and electronics stores, one of the largest in its niche in the country. It works both through large shop floors and through its online store. It sits in the same group of companies as the Eldorado chain.","fact":"The chain was one of the first in Russia to push appliance sales over the internet, back in the early 2000s.","wiki_en":"M.video","wiki_ru":"М.Видео"},{"name":"Eldorado","emoji":"📺","meta":["retail","Russia","1994"],"description":"A Russian chain of home appliance and electronics stores, known in the 2000s for mass advertising campaigns and low prices. The name points to the legendary land of gold from South American myth. It now works in the same group as the M.Video chain.","fact":"In the mid-2000s the chain found itself at the centre of one of the biggest tax scandals in Russian retail.","wiki_en":"Eldorado (retailer)","wiki_ru":"Эльдорадо (сеть магазинов)"},{"name":"Detsky Mir","emoji":"🧸","meta":["retail","Russia","1957"],"description":"A Russian chain of children's stores selling clothes, toys and goods for newborns. Its first and flagship shop opened in the very centre of Moscow and was for decades an emblem of Soviet childhood. It now trades across the country and in several neighbouring states.","fact":"The flagship store on Lubyanka Square in Moscow was for decades one of the largest children's department stores in Europe.","wiki_en":"Detsky Mir","wiki_ru":"Детский мир (сеть магазинов)"},{"name":"Sportmaster","emoji":"⚽","meta":["retail","Russia","1992"],"description":"A Russian chain of sporting goods stores, one of the largest in the country and in Eastern Europe. It sells international sports brands as well as its own labels of clothing and equipment. It grew out of a small firm trading in imported sports gear.","fact":"The chain has its own labels too — Demix and Outventure, for instance, which are sold only in its shops.","wiki_en":"Sportmaster","wiki_ru":"Спортмастер"},{"name":"Alfa-Bank","emoji":"🏦","meta":["banking","Russia","1990"],"description":"One of Russia's largest private banks, offering retail and corporate financial services. It is known for pushing digital services and mobile banking hard. It is part of the Alfa Group financial and industrial group.","fact":"The bank was one of the first in Russia to promote remote banking through the internet and a mobile app on a large scale.","wiki_en":"Alfa-Bank","wiki_ru":"Альфа-банк"},{"name":"VTB","emoji":"🏦","meta":["banking","Russia","1990"],"description":"One of Russia's largest banks, with state participation, offering the full range of banking and investment services. It works both with private customers and with the big corporate sector. The name stands for Vneshtorgbank, the foreign trade bank, later VTB Bank.","fact":"The bank was originally set up to handle the country's foreign trade operations, which is where its first name came from.","wiki_en":"VTB Bank"},{"name":"Raiffeisenbank","emoji":"🏦","meta":["banking","Russia","1996"],"description":"The Russian arm of the Austrian Raiffeisen banking group, one of the largest foreign-owned banks on the Russian market. Its lineage goes back to the 19th-century cooperative banking movement founded by Friedrich Raiffeisen in Germany. It serves private and corporate customers.","fact":"The bank's name goes back to the German public figure Friedrich Wilhelm Raiffeisen, founder of the first credit cooperatives.","wiki_en":"Raiffeisen Bank International","wiki_ru":"Райффайзенбанк"},{"name":"Russian Post","emoji":"📮","meta":["post and logistics","Russia","2002"],"description":"The state enterprise that carries the post across the whole of Russia, from the big cities to the remotest villages. In its present shape it dates from the early 2000s, though the country's postal service is far older. It delivers letters, parcels and money transfers.","fact":"The company runs one of the world's largest networks of post offices, reaching even the least accessible settlements in the country.","wiki_en":"Russian Post"},{"name":"MTS","emoji":"📱","meta":["telecoms","Russia","1993"],"description":"One of Russia's largest mobile operators, which also provides internet, television and digital services. It began work back in the 1990s as one of the country's first GSM operators. It is among the leaders of the telecoms market along with MegaFon and Beeline.","fact":"MTS stands for Mobile TeleSystems.","wiki_en":"MTS (telecommunications)"},{"name":"MegaFon","emoji":"📶","meta":["telecoms","Russia","1993"],"description":"One of Russia's largest mobile operators, known for the green of its brand. It originally traded under a different name and was renamed in the early 2000s. It provides mobile telephony, internet and digital services across the country.","fact":"Before the rebranding in 2002 the company was called North-West GSM.","wiki_en":"MegaFon"},{"name":"Beeline","emoji":"📞","meta":["telecoms","Russia","1992"],"description":"One of Russia's largest mobile operators, recognisable by its yellow and black stripes. It was one of the first mobile operators to start work in the country, in the early 1990s. It belongs to the company VimpelCom.","fact":"The striped yellow-and-black logo was designed by an international design agency, taking its cue from the image of a bee.","wiki_en":"Beeline (brand)"},{"name":"Rostelecom","emoji":"📡","meta":["telecoms","Russia","1993"],"description":"Russia's largest provider of fixed-line telephony, broadband internet and digital television, with state participation. It was formed by bringing the regional long-distance operators together. It also develops mobile services and services for state digital platforms.","fact":"The company runs one of the largest fibre-optic backbone networks in the country.","wiki_en":"Rostelecom"},{"name":"Mosfilm","emoji":"🎬","meta":["film industry","Russia","1924"],"description":"Russia's largest film studio and one of the oldest in Europe, where a great many classic Soviet and Russian films were made. It stands in Moscow and holds a vast stock of sets and props. Many of the outstanding directors of Soviet cinema worked here.","fact":"The studio keeps its own collection of vintage cars, which are used in period shoots.","wiki_en":"Mosfilm"},{"name":"Channel One","emoji":"📺","meta":["television","Russia","1995"],"description":"One of Russia's main federal television channels, on air since Soviet times under various names. It traces its history to the first channel of central Soviet television. It shows news, series, entertainment and current affairs programmes.","fact":"Until 2002 the channel was called ORT — Russian Public Television.","wiki_en":"Channel One Russia"},{"name":"NTV","emoji":"📺","meta":["television","Russia","1993"],"description":"A Russian federal television channel, one of the first private channels in post-Soviet Russia. It is known for its news programmes, its drama series and its crime coverage. It now belongs to the Gazprom-Media holding.","fact":"NTV never had an official expansion: the founders themselves offered various readings, from \"independent\" to \"our\" television.","wiki_en":"NTV (Russia)"},{"name":"RBC","emoji":"📈","meta":["media","Russia","1993"],"description":"A Russian media group specialising in business news, economic analysis and financial information. It includes a television channel, a news agency, a newspaper and a web portal. The name stands for RosBusinessConsulting.","fact":"The company began as an analytical agency supplying data on the state of the financial markets.","wiki_en":"RosBusinessConsulting","wiki_ru":"РБК (медиахолдинг)"},{"name":"Avito","emoji":"📱","meta":["internet services","Russia","2007"],"description":"Russia's largest online classifieds platform, covering goods and services, property, jobs and cars. It was founded by Swedish entrepreneurs and quickly became one of the most visited sites in the country. Both private sellers and companies post listings on it.","fact":"The service was founded by two Swedish entrepreneurs, Jonas Nordlander and Filip Engelbert, who came to Russia to do it.","wiki_en":"Avito.ru","wiki_ru":"Авито"},{"name":"HeadHunter","emoji":"💼","meta":["internet services","Russia","2000"],"description":"A Russian online platform for job hunting and recruitment, one of the largest in the country and the Russian-speaking world. It brings job seekers and employers together through databases of CVs and vacancies. It is also known by its short name, hh.ru.","fact":"The company was one of the first in Russia to turn job hunting and CV posting into a mass online service.","wiki_en":"HeadHunter (company)","wiki_ru":"HeadHunter"},{"name":"CDEK","emoji":"📦","meta":["logistics","Russia","2000"],"description":"A Russian logistics company specialising in parcel delivery within the country and abroad. Its name comes from an abbreviation of \"express courier delivery service\". It works through a wide network of pick-up points in Russian cities and other countries.","fact":"The company started with courier deliveries of documents in a single Siberian city and now operates internationally.","wiki_en":"SDEK","wiki_ru":"СДЭК"},{"name":"Dodo Pizza","emoji":"🍕","meta":["food service","Russia","2011"],"description":"A Russian pizza chain that grew from one pizzeria in Syktyvkar into an international franchise. It is known for showing the making of its pizzas openly through webcams in the kitchen. It also builds its own IT platform for managing orders.","fact":"The company was one of the first in the world to put webcams in pizzeria kitchens so that customers could watch their order being made online.","wiki_en":"Dodo Pizza"},{"name":"Vkusno & tochka","emoji":"🍔","meta":["food service","Russia","2022"],"description":"A Russian fast-food chain created out of the former McDonald's restaurants after that company left the Russian market. It kept most of the menu and the recipes under a new name and logo. It operates in hundreds of towns across the country.","fact":"The chain's new logo is a circle and two stripes, in which a burger and a portion of fries can be read.","wiki_en":"Vkusno & tochka","wiki_ru":"Вкусно — и точка"},{"name":"Severstal","emoji":"🏭","meta":["steel","Russia","1993"],"description":"One of Russia's largest steel and mining companies, with its head office in Cherepovets. It makes rolled steel for construction, engineering and other industries. It was formed on the base of a large Soviet metallurgical works.","fact":"The Cherepovets works from which the company grew poured its first iron back in the mid-1950s.","wiki_en":"Severstal"},{"name":"Surgutneftegas","emoji":"🛢️","meta":["oil and gas","Russia","1993"],"description":"One of Russia's largest oil and gas producers, working mainly in Western Siberia. It was formed by merging several enterprises of the oil and gas complex of the Surgut district. It is known for how closed its ownership structure and financial reporting are.","fact":"The company has long been famous for an extremely opaque ownership structure — the exact list of its shareholders is still argued over.","wiki_en":"Surgutneftegas"},{"name":"Tatneft","emoji":"🛢️","meta":["oil and gas","Russia","1950"],"description":"One of Russia's largest oil companies, producing mainly in Tatarstan. Its history starts with the discovery of large oil fields in the republic in the middle of the 20th century. Besides extraction it runs refining and a tyre business.","fact":"The company owns a large tyre plant of its own, which is unusual for an oil company.","wiki_en":"Tatneft"},{"name":"Novatek","emoji":"🔥","meta":["oil and gas","Russia","1994"],"description":"Russia's largest independent producer of natural gas, which also specialises in liquefied natural gas. It runs large projects to extract and export LNG from the Yamal peninsula. It is one of the key companies of the Russian energy sector.","fact":"The company runs one of the world's largest liquefied natural gas projects above the Arctic Circle.","wiki_en":"Novatek","wiki_ru":"Новатэк"},{"name":"Polyus","emoji":"🥇","meta":["mining","Russia","2006"],"description":"Russia's largest gold miner and one of the largest in the world by volume of gold produced. It works deposits in Siberia and the Far East. It was created by restructuring the gold assets of a large mining group.","fact":"One of the company's key deposits, Olimpiada, is among the largest gold ore deposits in the world.","wiki_en":"Polyus (company)"},{"name":"Alrosa","emoji":"💎","meta":["mining","Russia","1992"],"description":"The world's largest company by volume of diamonds mined, working deposits mainly in Yakutia. It was built on the base of the Soviet diamond industry. The state remains one of its key shareholders.","fact":"The name is an abbreviation of \"Diamonds of Russia-Sakha\", pointing to the company's Yakut origins.","wiki_en":"Alrosa"},{"name":"Kamaz","emoji":"🚛","meta":["vehicle manufacturing","Russia","1969"],"description":"Russia's largest maker of lorries, known too for its success in rally raids, the Dakar among them. The plant was built in the town of Naberezhnye Chelny in Soviet times as a giant of heavy engineering. It makes lorries, buses and special vehicles.","fact":"The Kamaz-Master racing team has won the truck class of the Dakar Rally many times over.","wiki_en":"Kamaz"},{"name":"GAZ","emoji":"🚚","meta":["vehicle manufacturing","Russia","1932"],"description":"One of Russia's oldest car plants, standing in Nizhny Novgorod. It began by building cars under licence from America's Ford and later moved to designs of its own. It made the famous Volga saloons and still builds the Gazelle light commercial vans.","fact":"The plant was built in the early 1930s with the direct involvement of American engineers and Ford equipment.","wiki_en":"GAZ"},{"name":"Sukhoi","emoji":"✈️","meta":["aviation","Russia","1939"],"description":"A Russian aircraft design bureau named after the designer Pavel Sukhoi. It develops military combat aircraft, including the Su family of fighters, as well as the civil Superjet airliner. It is one of the country's leading aviation firms.","fact":"Su fighters designed by the bureau are flown by the air forces of dozens of countries around the world.","wiki_en":"Sukhoi"},{"name":"MiG","emoji":"✈️","meta":["aviation","Russia","1939"],"description":"A Russian aircraft design bureau whose name is made from the surnames of its founders, Mikoyan and Gurevich. It develops the MiG fighters, known since Soviet times. It is one of the oldest and best-known aviation brands in the world.","fact":"The MiG-15 was among the first mass-produced jet fighters to meet in air combat, during the Korean War.","wiki_en":"Mikoyan"},{"name":"Tupolev","emoji":"✈️","meta":["aviation","Russia","1922"],"description":"A Russian aircraft design bureau founded by the aeronautical engineer Andrei Tupolev. It developed both military bombers and passenger aircraft, including the Tu-144, the world's first supersonic airliner. It is one of the oldest aviation firms in the country.","fact":"The Tu-144 flew before the French-British Concorde — on the last day of 1968, nearly two months ahead of its rival.","wiki_en":"Tupolev"},{"name":"Russian Helicopters","emoji":"🚁","meta":["aviation","Russia","2007"],"description":"A Russian holding company that brings together all the country's major helicopter builders, including the Mil and Kamov design bureaux. It designs and builds both military and civil helicopters. It is one of the world's key makers of rotorcraft.","fact":"The Mi and Ka helicopters gathered under this brand are flown in dozens of countries around the world.","wiki_en":"Russian Helicopters"},{"name":"RusHydro","emoji":"💧","meta":["energy","Russia","2004"],"description":"One of Russia's largest energy companies, specialising in hydroelectric power. It runs large hydroelectric stations across the country, including plants in Siberia and the Far East. It was created by the reform of the country's unified power system.","fact":"The company operates the Sayano-Shushenskaya dam — one of the largest hydroelectric stations in the world.","wiki_en":"RusHydro"},{"name":"Inter RAO","emoji":"⚡","meta":["energy","Russia","1997"],"description":"A Russian energy company that generates electricity and also exports and imports it across borders. It runs a number of large thermal power stations in different regions of the country. It plays a notable part in Russia's energy links with neighbouring states.","fact":"The company has historically specialised in exporting and importing electricity between Russia and the countries on its borders.","wiki_en":"Inter RAO"},{"name":"Yota","emoji":"📶","meta":["telecoms","Russia","2007"],"description":"A Russian mobile operator, known first as one of the pioneers of fourth-generation (4G/LTE) networks in the country. It later concentrated on unlimited mobile internet tariffs and a virtual SIM card. It is now part of MegaFon.","fact":"Yota was the first in Russia to launch a commercial LTE mobile internet network.","wiki_en":"Yota","wiki_ru":"Yota"},{"name":"1C","emoji":"💻","meta":["software","Russia","1991"],"description":"A Russian software developer best known for its 1C:Enterprise accounting and business systems, which became an industry standard in the country. It also published popular computer games in the 1990s and 2000s. It works through a wide network of implementation partners.","fact":"Besides business software, the company used to make and publish computer games, including localisations of foreign hits.","wiki_en":"1C Company","wiki_ru":"1С"},{"name":"SberMarket","emoji":"🛒","meta":["internet services","Russia","2013 (as Instamart)"],"description":"A Russian service that delivers groceries and supermarket goods to the door through a mobile app. It is part of Sber's ecosystem. It works with the big retail chains, picking and delivering orders for their customers.","fact":"The service grew out of the Instamart start-up, became SberMarket after Sber came in, and in 2024 changed its name to Kuper.","wiki_en":"SberMarket","wiki_ru":"СберМаркет"},{"name":"Yandex Market","emoji":"🛍️","meta":["internet services","Russia","2000"],"description":"A Russian platform for comparing prices and shopping in online stores, which later turned into a full marketplace. It started out as an aggregator of offers from different sellers. It is part of the Yandex company.","fact":"At first the service did not sell anything itself; it only helped compare prices between different online shops.","wiki_en":"Yandex Market"},{"name":"Cherkizovo","emoji":"🍗","meta":["food industry","Russia","1974"],"description":"One of Russia's largest agribusiness companies, producing poultry, pork and meat products. It is named after the Cherkizovo meat processing plant near Moscow, where the company's history began. Its products are sold under several of its own labels.","fact":"The company grew from a single Soviet meat processing plant into one of the country's largest agricultural holdings.","wiki_en":"Cherkizovo","wiki_ru":"Черкизово (компания)"},{"name":"Miratorg","emoji":"🥩","meta":["food industry","Russia","1995"],"description":"One of Russia's largest agribusiness groups, specialising in beef, pork and poultry. It runs the whole cycle, from raising livestock to processing and selling the finished product. It is one of the leaders of the country's meat market.","fact":"The company runs one of the largest beef cattle breeding projects in Europe.","wiki_en":"Miratorg"},{"name":"Baltika","emoji":"🍺","meta":["food industry","Russia","1990"],"description":"Russia's largest brewer, whose beers, labelled by number, are well known far beyond the country. The brewery was built in St Petersburg and quickly grew into one of the leaders of the Eastern European beer market. It exports beer to dozens of countries.","fact":"Baltika beer, in its various numbered varieties, was long one of Russia's most exported brands in the world.","wiki_en":"Baltika Breweries"},{"name":"Ochakovo","emoji":"🍺","meta":["food industry","Russia","1978"],"description":"A Russian brewer and soft drinks maker, named after the Moscow district where its plant stands. It makes beer, kvass and soft drinks, and remains one of the few large independent producers in the industry. It is one of the country's best-known makers of traditional kvass.","fact":"Ochakovo is one of the few large Russian brewers that does not belong to an international beer group.","wiki_en":"Ochakovo (company)","wiki_ru":"Очаково (компания)"},{"name":"Ostankino Meat Processing Plant","emoji":"🥓","meta":["food industry","Russia","Soviet era"],"description":"One of Russia's largest makers of meat products — sausages, frankfurters and prepared foods. Its name comes from the Ostankino district of Moscow, where the plant stands. Its products are widely stocked by retail chains across the country.","fact":"The plant takes its name from the old Moscow estate of Ostankino, which also gave its name to the district and to the television tower.","wiki_en":"Ostankino Meat Processing Plant","wiki_ru":"Останкинский мясоперерабатывающий комбинат"},{"name":"Domik v Derevne","emoji":"🥛","meta":["food industry","Russia","late 1990s"],"description":"A Russian dairy brand — milk, soured cream and more — packaged in a country-life style; the name means \"a little house in the village\". It belongs to the international company PepsiCo through its dairy arm. It is one of the recognisable dairy brands on the Russian market.","fact":"The name and the packaging deliberately point to the image of a traditional village farm.","wiki_en":"Domik v Derevne"},{"name":"Prostokvashino","emoji":"🐄","meta":["food industry","Russia","from the early 2000s"],"description":"A Russian dairy brand named after the village in a much-loved cartoon about the cat Matroskin and Uncle Fyodor. Made by a large dairy producer once part of the Danone group, the range covers milk, kefir, curd cheese and yoghurts. The packaging often uses the cartoon characters.","fact":"The name and characters refer to the Soviet cartoon \"Three from Prostokvashino\", though the company had nothing to do with making it.","wiki_en":"Prostokvashino (brand)","wiki_ru":"Простоквашино (молочная продукция)"}];
